# This folder contains figure 1, 2 and 3.
This README is a description of the three figures.

# Figure 1
This is nine wordclouds divided by the countries: Brazil (BRA), China (CHN), Deutschland/Federal Republic of Germany (DEU), 
France (FRA), Great Britain (GRB), Poland (POL), Russia/USSR (RUS), USA and Yugoslavia (YUG). The wordclouds show the most
common words for the countries in the period 1989-2000 after sorting. See our file: 'un_speeches_processing_and_figures.html' 
in Github for list of stopwords.
The biggest words are the ones used most pr. country in their total number of speeches in the period.

# Figure 2
The data in this figure is the same as in figure 1, but represented in a bar graph instead of a wordcloud. 
There are nine bar graphs, one for each country. The x-axis shows the number of times the word has appeared in the total speeches of the country in the period 1989-2000. 
The y-axis shows the most appearing words in the total speeches of the country in the period 1989-2000 after sorting.

# Figure 3
Shows term-frequency patterns in UN General Debate speeches, 1992–2000: “Historical_Process” sorted by country; 
“Liberal Democracy” by country and by year; and “end of history” by country. Figure 3 covers 1992–2000 rather than the full 1989–2000 period because
The End of History and the Last Man was published in 1992. 
References to the book itself would therefore not be expected before its publication.


